sections.content:
An `if` statement allows your program to execute code only when a condition is true.

```js
if (condition) {
  // code to run
}
```

The condition inside the parentheses should produce a boolean result.

For example:

```js
const health = 0;

if (health <= 0) {
  console.log("Game Over");
}
```

JavaScript evaluates `health <= 0`. Because the result is `true`, the code inside the block runs.

This creates one of the most important patterns in programming:

**Evaluate a condition → choose whether code should run.**

The braces `{}` define the block of code controlled by the condition. Keeping related code inside clear blocks makes programs easier to understand and maintain.

examples.code:
const health = 75;

if (health > 0) {
  console.log("Player is alive");
}

sections.content:
An `if` statement handles one condition, but programs often need to handle alternative outcomes.

Use `else` when you want code to run when the `if` condition is false.

```js
if (score >= 50) {
  console.log("Passed");
} else {
  console.log("Failed");
}
```

When there are several possible conditions, use `else if`.

```js
if (score >= 90) {
  console.log("Excellent");
} else if (score >= 50) {
  console.log("Passed");
} else {
  console.log("Failed");
}
```

JavaScript checks the conditions from top to bottom. Once it finds a condition that is true, it executes that block and skips the remaining branches.

The order of your conditions therefore matters.

examples.code:
const score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
  console.log("Gold");
} else if (score >= 70) {
  console.log("Silver");
} else {
  console.log("Bronze");
}

const username = "Alex";

if (username) {
  console.log("Welcome, " + username);
}

sections.content:
Knowing the operators is only part of becoming a good developer. You also need to write conditions that other developers can understand.

Compare these examples:

```js
if (score >= 50) {
  console.log("Passed");
}
```

and:

```js
if (!(score < 50)) {
  console.log("Passed");
}
```

Both can represent the same idea, but the first is easier to read.

Prefer conditions that directly express the business rule. Use meaningful variable names and avoid unnecessarily complicated expressions.

For example:

```js
const hasPermission = true;
const isLoggedIn = true;

if (isLoggedIn && hasPermission) {
  console.log("Access granted");
}
```

Clear code is not just about making the computer understand your program. It is also about making your code understandable to the people who maintain it.

