sections.content:
An array is a data structure used to store multiple values in a single variable. Arrays are useful when you need to work with a collection of related values such as player names, game scores, products, or inventory items.

Instead of creating separate variables for every item:

```js
const item1 = "Sword";
const item2 = "Shield";
const item3 = "Potion";
```

You can group them into one array:

```js
const inventory = ["Sword", "Shield", "Potion"];
```

Arrays are ordered collections, which means every element has a position called an **index**.

You can change an existing element by assigning a new value to its index.

```js
const inventory = ["Sword", "Shield", "Potion"];

inventory[1] = "Armor";

console.log(inventory);
```

The array now contains `Sword`, `Armor`, and `Potion`.

Although `inventory` was declared using `const`, its elements can still be changed. `const` prevents reassignment of the variable itself; it does not make the contents of an array immutable.

The `pop()` method removes the last element from an array.

```js
const inventory = ["Sword", "Shield", "Potion"];

const removedItem = inventory.pop();

console.log(removedItem);
console.log(inventory);
```

`pop()` returns the element that was removed. This makes it useful when you need both to modify the array and know which value was removed.

Arrays are often processed using loops. A `for` loop allows you to visit each element one by one.

```js
const scores = [100, 200, 300];

for (let i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
  console.log(scores[i]);
}
```

The loop starts at index `0` and continues until it reaches the end of the array.

This pattern is useful for processing every item in a collection.

Arrays are used throughout modern applications. A game can use arrays for inventories and scores. An e-commerce application can use them for products in a cart. A social media application can use them for posts or followers.

```js
const cart = [
  "Gaming Mouse",
  "Keyboard",
  "Headset"
];

console.log(cart.length);
```

Understanding arrays is important because many real-world APIs return collections of data as arrays.

