sections.content:
Objects are used to group related information together using **key-value pairs**. They are useful for representing real-world entities such as players, users, products, or orders.

For example, instead of storing player information in separate variables:

```js
const playerName = "Shadow";
const playerLevel = 25;
const playerHealth = 100;
```

You can group the information into one object:

```js
const player = {
  name: "Shadow",
  level: 25,
  health: 100
};
```

Each piece of information is stored as a property.

Objects are created using curly braces `{}`. Each property contains a key and a value separated by a colon.

```js
const player = {
  name: "Shadow",
  level: 25,
  health: 100
};
```

The keys are `name`, `level`, and `health`. Their corresponding values are `"Shadow"`, `25`, and `100`.

Properties are separated by commas.

There are two common ways to access object properties: **dot notation** and **bracket notation**.

Dot notation is the most common:

```js
const player = {
  name: "Shadow",
  level: 25
};

console.log(player.name);
```

You can also use bracket notation:

```js
console.log(player["name"]);
```

Both approaches access the same property.

You can add a new property to an object or change an existing property using assignment.

```js
const player = {
  name: "Shadow",
  level: 25
};

player.health = 100;
player.level = 26;

console.log(player);
```

Just like arrays, an object declared with `const` can have its properties modified. `const` prevents reassignment of the object variable itself.

The `delete` operator can remove a property from an object.

```js
const player = {
  name: "Shadow",
  level: 25,
  temporaryBonus: 50
};

delete player.temporaryBonus;

console.log(player);
```

The `temporaryBonus` property is removed from the object.

In real applications, deletion should be used intentionally because other parts of the program may expect a property to exist.

Object properties can store different types of values, including strings, numbers, booleans, arrays, and even other objects.

```js
const player = {
  name: "Shadow",
  level: 25,
  isOnline: true,
  inventory: ["Sword", "Potion"]
};
```

This makes objects useful for representing complex entities.

An object can contain another object as a property. This is called a nested object.

```js
const player = {
  name: "Shadow",
  stats: {
    health: 100,
    attack: 50
  }
};

console.log(player.stats.health);
```

Nested objects allow related information to be grouped into logical sections.

A very common pattern in JavaScript is storing multiple objects inside an array.

```js
const players = [
  { name: "Alex", level: 10 },
  { name: "Sam", level: 15 },
  { name: "John", level: 20 }
];
```

You can access an object using its array index and then access one of its properties:

```js
console.log(players[0].name);
```

This pattern is extremely common when working with data received from APIs.

Objects are one of the most important structures in JavaScript applications. A user profile, product, game character, order, or API response can all be represented using objects.

```js
const product = {
  name: "Gaming Mouse",
  price: 2499,
  inStock: true
};

console.log(product.name);
console.log(product.price);
```

Understanding objects is essential before working with APIs, databases, React state, and more advanced JavaScript.

