sections.content:
The `forEach()` method runs a function once for every element in an array.

```js
const players = ["Alex", "Sam", "John"];

players.forEach((player) => {
  console.log(player);
});
```

The callback function receives each element as the array is processed.

`forEach()` is useful when you want to perform an action for every element and do not need to create a new array.

The `map()` method creates a **new array** by transforming every element in the original array.

```js
const scores = [10, 20, 30];

const doubled = scores.map((score) => {
  return score * 2;
});

console.log(doubled);
```

The result is `[20, 40, 60]`.

`map()` is useful when you want to transform every item into a new value.

The `filter()` method creates a new array containing only the elements that satisfy a condition.

```js
const scores = [30, 55, 80, 40, 90];

const passingScores = scores.filter((score) => {
  return score >= 50;
});

console.log(passingScores);
```

The result is `[55, 80, 90]`.

`filter()` is useful when you need to select specific items from a collection.

The `find()` method returns the **first element** that satisfies a condition.

```js
const players = [
  { name: "Alex", level: 10 },
  { name: "Sam", level: 20 },
  { name: "John", level: 30 }
];

const player = players.find((player) => {
  return player.level >= 20;
});

console.log(player);
```

The method stops searching once it finds the first matching element.

If no element matches the condition, `find()` returns `undefined`.

The `reduce()` method processes an array and combines its elements into a single result.

For example, you can use it to calculate a total:

```js
const prices = [100, 200, 300];

const total = prices.reduce((sum, price) => {
  return sum + price;
}, 0);

console.log(total);
```

The result is `600`.

`reduce()` is powerful because the final result does not have to be another array. It can be a number, string, object, or another value.

Array methods are heavily used when processing data from APIs and databases.

For example, an application might receive a list of products and display only products that are currently available:

```js
const products = [
  { name: "Mouse", available: true },
  { name: "Keyboard", available: false },
  { name: "Headset", available: true }
];

const availableProducts = products.filter((product) => {
  return product.available;
});

console.log(availableProducts);
```

Being comfortable with array methods is an important step toward writing modern JavaScript code.

